---

<section aria-labelledby="responses">
  <h2 id="responses">Orbital and spin responses</h2>
  <p>
    Diamagnetism arises from field-induced orbital motion and is present even without permanent moments. Paramagnetism aligns pre-existing or itinerant spin/orbital moments against thermal disorder. The source develops Langevin, Curie, and Pauli limits to show why localized moments and a degenerate electron gas produce different temperature dependence.
  </p>
  <p>
    These susceptibilities are response coefficients. A positive susceptibility does not by itself establish spontaneous magnetic order, and a fitted moment does not uniquely identify an atomic oxidation or spin state.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="localized-order">
  <h2 id="localized-order">Localized moments and exchange models</h2>
  <p>
    When moments remain well defined on sites, a Heisenberg model compresses their low-energy interaction into pair couplings:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Heisenberg spin Hamiltonian">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>·</mo><msub><mover><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&#92;hat H=-&#92;frac12&#92;sum_&#123;i&#92;ne j&#125;J_&#123;ij&#125;&#92;hat&#123;&#92;mathbf S&#125;_i&#92;cdot&#92;hat&#123;&#92;mathbf S&#125;_j</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The sign convention shown makes positive J favor parallel alignment; other texts reverse the sign, so the Hamiltonian must accompany any quoted J. The model presumes a stable moment magnitude and places dynamics mainly in orientation. Exchange couplings can be direct, superexchange, or mediated by itinerant electrons, and their range matters.
  </p>
  <p class="paper-meta">Source locator: Cohen &amp; Louie, Chapter 15, §15.4. The displayed sign convention is declared explicitly because conventions vary.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="spin-waves">
  <h2 id="spin-waves">Collective motion of an ordered state</h2>
  <p>
    A spin wave is a coherent spatial twist of magnetic order. Quantization gives magnons, just as quantizing lattice normal modes gives phonons. The analogy is useful but not identity: the displaced object is the order parameter or spin configuration, and spin–orbit coupling can gap or mix modes that would otherwise be symmetry-protected.
  </p>
  <p>
    A calculated Γ-point mode does not prove stability for every magnon wavevector, just as a Γ phonon does not establish full-q lattice stability.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="itinerant">
  <h2 id="itinerant">Itinerant magnetism is a different limit</h2>
  <p>
    In an itinerant metal, spin polarization reorganizes extended electronic occupations rather than merely orienting rigid local spins. The Stoner criterion balances band-energy cost against an exchange gain and helps explain why a high DOS at the Fermi level can favor polarization. It remains a mean-field instability test, not a complete theory of fluctuating or strongly correlated magnetism.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mapping an itinerant calculation onto localized J<sub>ij</sub> parameters is an additional model reduction whose validity must be tested.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="impurity">
  <h2 id="impurity">Magnetic impurities and the Kondo boundary</h2>
  <p>
    A localized impurity moment coupled antiferromagnetically to conduction electrons can be screened below a Kondo scale. The logarithmic resistivity trend and susceptibility crossover expose many-body physics that static mean field cannot reproduce. The screened ground state is not simply “the moment disappeared”; it is an entangled state of impurity and bath.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="calculation-claims">
  <h2 id="calculation-claims">What a spin-polarized calculation can establish</h2>
  <p>
    A converged collinear calculation establishes a stationary solution within its functional, structure, cell, spin constraint, and initialization. It does not prove the global magnetic ground state. Competing ferro-, antiferro-, ferri-, noncollinear, and orbital branches may need separate tests; scalar relativity, spin polarization, noncollinearity, and spin–orbit coupling are distinct model choices.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use <a href={href('/theory/relativistic-electronic-structure-spin-and-magnetism/')}>Foundations · Relativistic Electronic Structure, Spin, and Magnetism</a> for deeper repair and the workflow layer for branch searches and evidence.
  </p>
</section>
